          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.817,
            0.75,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In eukaryotic cells, a network of fine fibres forms the cytoskeleton, which provides structural support, maintains cell shape, and enables cell movement and internal transport. It is visible only under an electron microscope as a separate entity. The cytoplasm may also store starch (in plant cells), or crystals of calcium oxalate or silica (in some plant cells). These are known as cell inclusions."
            }
          ]
        },
